BEGIN_PGML The point [`(4,-5)`] is on the graph in Figure[$NBSP]*3.2.3(c). What happens when you substitute these values into the equation [`y=-2x+3\text{?}`]
>> [@DataTable( [ [ [PGML('[`y`]'), rowbottom => '2', midrule => '1',], PGML('[`=`]'), PGML('[`-2x+3`]'), ], [ PGML('[_]{$y}{2}'), PGML('[`\wonder{=}`]'), PGML('[`-2(`][_]{4}{2}[`)+3`]'), ], [ PGML('[_]{$y}{2}'), PGML('[`\wonder{=}`]'), PGML('[_]{$f}{10}'), ], ], align => 'rcl', valign => 'middle',);@]* <<
[_]{$truefalse}{5}
END_PGML
########## # Solution ##########
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION Each side of the equation simplifies to [`-5\text{.}`] In other words, the equation simplifies to an outright true equation.
